replace_section("## 3.10 Preregistration", """
## 3.10 Preregistration As A Working Method Including Where It Failed

The pattern used throughout the post-lock work is uniform: the criteria, tolerances and stop rule for a run are written into the driver script's docstring and committed **before the run**, the grading is mechanical, failure is reported rather than iterated away, and the interpretive direction is registered too — for the acoustic protocol test, failure meant *the protocol is not confirmed*, never *the model is wrong*. Two worked examples show the machinery catching a defect in a **rule** and a defect in the **analyst**.

**A pre-registered check caught the analyst's own bug.** The probe of the referential deficit index registered a provenance check with nothing to do with the hypothesis — the recomputed pronoun-to-noun ratio must reproduce the committed feature to within one part in a million on at least 99% of recordings, failing which the run is void — and **it failed**, reproducing the committed feature on 36% of Pittsburgh and 82% of Delaware recordings. The defect was in the **new script**, not the tagger, the environment or the committed extractor (Appendix H), and fixing it **honoured** the pre-registration rather than relaxing it; one cohort's result had appeared in terminal output before the fix, and that is recorded in the driver's registration history in those words. The check that caught it was a provenance formality nobody expected to fail: the value of pre-registration is concentrated in the checks written when the author is not worried.

**A criterion that failed on contact with the data.** The cross-corpus transfer analysis registered a retention ratio — transfer AUC over the within-corpus reference of the test corpus — which silently assumes its denominator is a working reference, and in one direction it is not: Delaware trained on itself scores 0.5474, an interval spanning chance (section 5.12.1), so the ratio came out at 1.18 and the registered rule stamped it **strong**, a meaningless verdict. **The criterion was left exactly as registered and the arm was not re-run**, because **a rule rewritten after seeing the result is the result wearing a rule's clothes**; the first criterion, that transfer exceeds chance, passed on its own terms, so the transfer finding stands and only the retention framing is void. **Neither failure was found by a reviewer; both were found by the machinery, which is the only test of whether the machinery is real.**

Three registrations in seventeen runs required amendment on contact with the data — the retention criterion above, the tokenisation of the referential-index probe, and the label rule of the task-count curve, where 6.25% of participants change label across visits — and Appendix H tabulates each with its timing relative to seeing the result. They share a shape: in none of the three was the defect in the hypothesis, the criterion band or the analysis set, the three things a registration template asks for. **A pre-registration protects the parts of a design its author already knows are contestable; it does not protect the parts they have not thought of, and in practice those are where the defects are.** The working discipline this produced is four rules, derived in Appendix H: never adjust the rule to reach the assertion; record when an amendment was made relative to seeing the result; report both versions whenever an amendment changes an answer; and register a sanity gate on every derived quantity an analysis depends on, including its labels. A fourth case extends the last rule to comparisons: a claim that a hand-countable feature matched the calibrated ensemble **reversed sign once both sides were estimated alike** (section N5.16), because one side had been selected as the best of 43 on the same participants and the other not selected at all — so the rule extends to refusing any comparison whose sides were produced by procedures with different numbers of free parameters.

---
""", upto="## 3.11 Monotone")

